# Story 10.5 (AD-14 Pattern B deviation): standalone Containerfile for the
# DB-GPT sidecar -- DB-GPT's own model-worker + FastAPI webserver process,
# run as its own container rather than mounted in-process into the Django
# host. Contrast `src/platform/Containerfile` (Story 10.3): that image
# materializes the shared `python-agent-platform` env, where Langflow still
# runs in-process (Pattern A, unaffected by this deviation). This file
# mirrors that Containerfile's own PIXI MATERIALIZATION PATTERN -- a pixi
# builder stage -> a minimal, non-root, arbitrary-UID-compatible runtime
# stage -- but sources from the ISOLATED `dbgpt-sidecar` pixi
# feature/environment (pixi.toml) instead. That isolation is the entire
# reason this file (and Pattern B) exists: `dbgpt-app` transitively requires
# `dbgpt-client`, which pins `fastapi >=0.100.0,<0.113.0`; the shared
# `python-agent-platform` env already carries `langflow-base`'s
# `fastapi >=0.135.0,<1.0.0` floor (Story 11.1). The two ranges are
# disjoint -- confirmed live by attempting to add `dbgpt-app` to
# `python-agent-platform` and watching the solve fail with exactly this
# conflict -- which is the demonstrated pluggability failure
# `docs/dreams/db-gpt-django-plugin.md`'s 2026-08-21 AD-14 entry records.
# See `pixi.toml`'s own `[feature.dbgpt-sidecar]` comment for the full
# citation; not re-derived here.
#
# BUILD CONTEXT IS THE REPO ROOT, not this directory -- exactly like
# `src/platform/Containerfile`, and for the identical reason: the builder
# stage's `pixi install --frozen -e dbgpt-sidecar` needs the workspace-root
# `pixi.toml`/`pixi.lock`, which do not exist under
# `src/platform/compose/dbgpt/`. The working invocation is:
#   docker build -f src/platform/compose/dbgpt/Containerfile -t dbgpt-sidecar-test .
# `src/platform/compose/` is entirely `.dockerignore`d (Story 10.3's own
# addition -- that image has no use for the local compose stack), which is
# why this Containerfile never `COPY`s anything from its own directory: every
# file it needs either lives at the repo root (`pixi.toml`/`pixi.lock`,
# reached through the builder stage's full `COPY . /app`) or is generated
# in-place by a `RUN printf` (this file's own config + entrypoint, mirroring
# how `src/platform/Containerfile` generates ITS `entrypoint.sh` -- fixed
# literals reach a build no `COPY` from the ignored tree ever could).
#
# --platform=linux/amd64 pinned on every stage for the same reason as both
# existing Containerfiles in this repo (Story 7.1, Story 10.3): the
# `dbgpt-sidecar` feature's own `platforms = ["linux-64"]` (pixi.toml) has no
# osx-arm64/linux-aarch64 entry -- this env is container-build-only, never a
# native local-dev target, so it was never given one.
#
# This tag must track pixi.toml's own `requires-pixi` floor. It went
# unregistered in the pin-site list for its entire life until 2026-08-21,
# when a real `pixi upgrade` (0.76.2 -> 0.77.0) left it stale and broke
# `container-dbgpt` CI on the very next push -- nothing had ever pointed
# here. Now registered in `scripts/pixi_version_registry.py`:
# `pixi run -e local-recipes pixi-version-check` fails CI if this drifts
# again, and `bump-pixi-version` is how you change it.
FROM --platform=linux/amd64 ghcr.io/prefix-dev/pixi:0.80.0 AS builder

# WORKDIR /app -- same short, fixed path as both existing Containerfiles in
# this repo (worktree tooling has a documented path-length panic in
# pixi-build-backends past a ~173-byte workspace root; see the root
# Containerfile's own comment for the full citation).
WORKDIR /app

# Full repo checkout, not a cherry-picked subset -- same rationale as
# `src/platform/Containerfile`'s own `COPY . /app`: this builder stage needs
# (a) the `dbgpt-sidecar` env itself and (b) a SECOND, build-time-only env
# (`pyforge-steward`) for the secrets-scan gate below. Both are discarded
# from the final image -- see the runtime stage's narrow `COPY --from=builder`.
COPY . /app

# .pixi/config.toml is tracked and load-bearing but deliberately NOT reached
# via the COPY above -- see the root Containerfile's identical comment for
# the full reasoning (retro action item 9, 2026-09-05).
RUN mkdir -p .pixi && printf 'run-post-link-scripts = "insecure"\n' > .pixi/config.toml

# --frozen: install EXACTLY what pixi.lock says (same convention as both
# existing Containerfiles).
RUN pixi install --frozen -e dbgpt-sidecar

# Activation script for `dbgpt-sidecar` -- sourced by the runtime
# entrypoint below so the `dbgpt` CLI lands on PATH without shipping the
# pixi binary itself into the runtime stage.
RUN pixi shell-hook -e dbgpt-sidecar -s bash > /app/shell-hook.sh

# Story 7.3 ("Credentials never enter image layers") gate, reused verbatim
# (AD-2 -- no second scanner). Same deviation as `src/platform/Containerfile`
# from the spec's literal "final stage" wording, for the identical reason
# that file's own comment documents in full (not re-derived here): `steward`
# must never become a dependency of the shipped env, so it is materialized
# as a separate, build-time-only env in THIS stage and never copied forward.
# SCOPE is narrower here than the main image's: this Containerfile never
# `COPY`s any human-authored tree into the runtime stage (no
# `src/platform/`-style app code -- see the top-of-file comment on why this
# file has nothing under its own directory to ship), so the pixi-generated
# `shell-hook.sh` is the entire scan surface. The runtime stage's own
# generated `entrypoint.sh` and sidecar config are FIXED printf literals
# with no build-time interpolation (same category `src/platform/Containerfile`
# already excludes its own `entrypoint.sh` from scanning for) and so are not
# re-scanned here either.
RUN pixi install --frozen -e pyforge-steward
RUN pixi shell-hook -e pyforge-steward -s bash > /app/steward-shell-hook.sh
RUN bash -c "source /app/steward-shell-hook.sh \
    && python3 /app/scripts/container-gates secrets-scan /app/shell-hook.sh"

FROM --platform=linux/amd64 registry.access.redhat.com/ubi9/ubi-minimal:9.6 AS runtime

# WORKDIR /app -- same absolute path as the builder stage, required because
# `pixi shell-hook`'s output bakes ABSOLUTE paths into
# `/app/.pixi/envs/dbgpt-sidecar/...` (same reason `src/platform/Containerfile`
# gives for its own identical WORKDIR).
WORKDIR /app

# `util-linux` (NOT `util-linux-core` -- confirmed live: the `-core` subset
# omits `lscpu`) -- UBI9-minimal ships neither. `dbgpt.util.system_utils.
# get_cpu_avx_support()` unconditionally `subprocess.run(["lscpu"], ...)` on
# any Linux host, no try/except, no fallback -- this runs on every webserver
# boot (startup banner's system-info collection), so without it the
# container crashes immediately with `FileNotFoundError: [Errno 2] No such
# file or directory: 'lscpu'` (reproduced live). This is `dbgpt` itself, not
# `dbgpt-app`'s own packaging -- unrelated to the `pilot_template` gap
# documented below.
RUN microdnf install -y util-linux && microdnf clean all

# Only the materialized env + its activation hook -- not the whole checkout
# the builder stage happens to contain.
COPY --from=builder /app/.pixi/envs/dbgpt-sidecar /app/.pixi/envs/dbgpt-sidecar
COPY --from=builder /app/shell-hook.sh /app/shell-hook.sh

# Same `exec -- "$@"` pattern (and the same reason) as both existing
# Containerfiles' entrypoints: without the `--` terminator, a leading-dash
# CMD override would be parsed as bash's OWN option instead of being handed
# to `exec`.
RUN printf '#!/bin/bash\nset -e\nsource /app/shell-hook.sh\nexec -- "$@"\n' > /app/entrypoint.sh \
    && chmod +x /app/entrypoint.sh

# $HOME is a DEDICATED SUBDIRECTORY, same pattern (and the same POSIX-unlink
# reasoning) as `src/platform/Containerfile`'s own `$HOME` block -- see that
# file's comment for the full argument (not re-derived here): a
# group-writable `/app` would let the runtime user replace `/app/entrypoint.sh`
# itself, since unlink/rename permission comes from the DIRECTORY, not the
# file. DB-GPT writes its own state under `$DBGPT_HOME` (default
# `~/.dbgpt/workspace` -- `dbgpt/configs/model_config.py::_detect_root_path`,
# confirmed live) whenever `DBGPT_HOME` is unset, so pointing `$HOME` at a
# writable subdirectory is sufficient without a separate `DBGPT_HOME` env --
# the sqlite metadata db, generated `pilot/` scaffold, and logs all land
# under `$HOME/.dbgpt/workspace` for free.
RUN mkdir -p /app/.home
ENV HOME=/app/.home
# Libro stays off: `dbgpt_serve.libro.service.Service.after_start` only
# Popen(["libro", ...]) when NOTE_BOOK_ENABLE is true (default). Without
# the CLI the webserver still comes Ready and prints
# `start libro exception！[Errno 2] ... 'libro'`. False skips that path.
ENV NOTE_BOOK_ENABLE=false

# RUNTIME CWD IS ALSO /app/.home, NOT /app -- a THIRD hard requirement,
# distinct from the two the config block below documents:
# `dbgpt_serve.evaluate`'s `BenchmarkDataManager` (registered unconditionally
# during `initialize_components`, not gated by any feature flag) defaults
# `cache_dir` to the bare relative string `"cache"`
# (`dbgpt_serve/evaluate/service/fetchdata/benchmark_data_manager.py`) and
# `os.makedirs()`s it eagerly in `__init__` -- resolved against the
# PROCESS'S CWD, not `$HOME`/`$DBGPT_HOME`. With the default `WORKDIR /app`
# (root:root 0755, deliberately not group-writable -- see the `$HOME`
# comment above) this crashes with `PermissionError: [Errno 13] Permission
# denied: 'cache'` on every boot (reproduced live). Re-pointing the runtime
# WORKDIR at the one directory this image already makes group-writable
# fixes it without loosening `/app` itself, and covers any other
# undocumented relative-path assumption the same way. Every earlier `COPY`/
# `RUN` above already ran against `/app` before this reassignment, so
# nothing upstream of this line is affected.
WORKDIR /app/.home

# MISSING `pilot_template/` ALEMBIC SCAFFOLD -- the `dbgpt-app` 0.8.1
# conda-forge package (recipes/db-gpt/recipe.yaml, delivered upstream via PR
# #33883) does not ship the `dbgpt_app/pilot_template/` directory its own
# `dbgpt_app/initialization/workspace_provisioning.py` expects to find and
# copy into `$HOME/.dbgpt/workspace/pilot/` on first run (both the webserver
# boot path AND `dbgpt db migration init` go through this -- confirmed live,
# there is no non-interactive startup path that avoids it). Root cause,
# traced into the installed package: upstream DB-GPT's own
# `packages/dbgpt-app/hatch_build.py` only populates that directory when
# hatchling builds a wheel FROM AN ALREADY-EXTRACTED SDIST (it
# force-includes `pilot/*` into the sdist as `pilot_tpl/*` via
# `pyproject.toml`, then this build hook remaps `pilot_tpl/*` ->
# `dbgpt_app/pilot_template/*` for the wheel) -- but this recipe's build
# script runs `pip install ./packages/dbgpt-app --no-deps
# --no-build-isolation`, which builds the wheel DIRECTLY from the source
# checkout, skipping the sdist step entirely, so `pilot_tpl/` never exists
# and the hook's own `os.path.exists()` guard silently no-ops. Without this
# directory the server crashes on first boot with `ImportError: Can't find
# Python file .../pilot/meta_data/alembic/env.py` (reproduced live). This is
# an upstream/recipe packaging gap, not a design choice -- carried as
# deferred work (a fix belongs in `recipes/db-gpt/recipe.yaml`, e.g.
# building via `python -m build --sdist` first, and is
# conda-forge-expert-skill territory, out of this story's scope).
#
# THE FIX HERE RESTORES EXACTLY WHAT UPSTREAM'S OWN PACKAGING INTENDED TO
# SHIP, rather than working around the crash by disabling alembic (which was
# tried first and rejected: `disable_alembic_upgrade = true` skips
# `_ddl_init_and_upgrade` but ALSO skips DB-GPT's own `db.create_all()` --
# both are gated behind the SAME flag in `dbgpt_app/base.py::
# _migration_db_storage` -- which left the metadata store with zero tables
# and moved the crash one step later, to `no such table: dbgpt_serve_flow`
# during the flow-DAG preload at startup; reproduced live). The four files
# below are copied VERBATIM from DB-GPT v0.8.1's own
# `pilot/meta_data/{alembic.ini,alembic/{README,env.py,script.py.mako}}`
# (MIT-licensed, same source this recipe already builds from) into the
# exact `dbgpt_app/pilot_template/meta_data/...` layout
# `workspace_provisioning.py` expects -- generic Alembic `env.py`/
# `script.py.mako`/`alembic.ini` boilerplate (`env.py`'s only
# DB-GPT-specific content is wiring `target_metadata`/`engine` to DB-GPT's
# own `dbgpt.storage.metadata.db_manager.db` object, load-bearing for
# `autogenerate` to see DB-GPT's real models -- the generic
# `alembic init`-shipped template alone resolves `target_metadata = None`
# and silently generates an EMPTY migration, verified by diffing against
# alembic's own `templates/generic/env.py`). With this directory present,
# DB-GPT's OWN unmodified alembic pipeline runs end-to-end on first boot --
# `_ensure_pilot_workspace` provisions the workspace, `create_migration_
# script` autogenerates a real revision, `upgrade_database` applies it --
# and every table (including `dbgpt_serve_flow`) exists before the flow-DAG
# preload runs. Reproduced live end-to-end: default `disable_alembic_upgrade
# = false` (i.e. omitted from the config below), clean boot, `/api/health`
# returns 200, `sqlite3 .../dbgpt.db .tables` lists `dbgpt_serve_flow` among
# 18 real tables.
#
# "VERBATIM" IS NOW ACTUALLY VERIFIED, not just asserted: all four printf
# blocks below are byte-for-byte diffed (2026-08-21) against
# https://raw.githubusercontent.com/eosphoros-ai/DB-GPT/v0.8.1/pilot/meta_data/
# {alembic.ini,alembic/{README,env.py,script.py.mako}} -- an earlier revision
# of this RUN step only `touch`ed an empty README and hand-typed abbreviated
# versions of the other three (comments/docstrings stripped), which
# contradicted this comment's own "copied VERBATIM" claim; caught in review.
# `env.py`'s `run_migrations_online()` SQLite branch opens a second
# connection (`context.configure(connection=engine.connect(), ...)`) instead
# of reusing the already-open `connection` variable from the `with
# engine.connect() as connection:` above it -- that is exactly what upstream
# ships, confirmed by this same byte-for-byte diff, not a bug this file's own
# generation introduced.
# Single shell-form RUN (one `/bin/sh -c` layer -- no nested `bash -c "..."`
# wrapper, deliberately: that pattern needs double-escaping `$`/`"` through
# two shell layers to land literally in the generated files, which is
# exactly the kind of thing that reads clean and is wrong in a way `docker
# build` won't catch. Calling the pixi env's `python` by its full path below
# needs no activation, so there is nothing here that actually requires
# `source`/`bash`.
RUN DBGPT_APP_DIR=$(/app/.pixi/envs/dbgpt-sidecar/bin/python -c "import dbgpt_app, os; print(os.path.dirname(dbgpt_app.__file__))") \
    && mkdir -p "$DBGPT_APP_DIR/pilot_template/meta_data/alembic" \
    && printf '%s\n' \
        'Generic single-database configuration.' \
        > "$DBGPT_APP_DIR/pilot_template/meta_data/alembic/README" \
    && printf '%s\n' \
        '# A generic, single database configuration.' \
        '' \
        '[alembic]' \
        '# path to migration scripts' \
        'script_location = alembic' \
        '' \
        '# template used to generate migration file names; The default value is %%(rev)s_%%(slug)s' \
        '# Uncomment the line below if you want the files to be prepended with date and time' \
        '# see https://alembic.sqlalchemy.org/en/latest/tutorial.html#editing-the-ini-file' \
        '# for all available tokens' \
        '# file_template = %%(year)d_%%(month).2d_%%(day).2d_%%(hour).2d%%(minute).2d-%%(rev)s_%%(slug)s' \
        '' \
        '# sys.path path, will be prepended to sys.path if present.' \
        '# defaults to the current working directory.' \
        'prepend_sys_path = .' \
        '' \
        '# timezone to use when rendering the date within the migration file' \
        '# as well as the filename.' \
        '# If specified, requires the python-dateutil library that can be' \
        '# installed by adding `alembic[tz]` to the pip requirements' \
        '# string value is passed to dateutil.tz.gettz()' \
        '# leave blank for localtime' \
        '# timezone =' \
        '' \
        '# max length of characters to apply to the' \
        '# "slug" field' \
        '# truncate_slug_length = 40' \
        '' \
        '# set to '\''true'\'' to run the environment during' \
        '# the '\''revision'\'' command, regardless of autogenerate' \
        '# revision_environment = false' \
        '' \
        '# set to '\''true'\'' to allow .pyc and .pyo files without' \
        '# a source .py file to be detected as revisions in the' \
        '# versions/ directory' \
        '# sourceless = false' \
        '' \
        '# version location specification; This defaults' \
        '# to alembic/versions.  When using multiple version' \
        '# directories, initial revisions must be specified with --version-path.' \
        '# The path separator used here should be the separator specified by "version_path_separator" below.' \
        '# version_locations = %(here)s/bar:%(here)s/bat:alembic/versions' \
        '' \
        '# version path separator; As mentioned above, this is the character used to split' \
        '# version_locations. The default within new alembic.ini files is "os", which uses os.pathsep.' \
        '# If this key is omitted entirely, it falls back to the legacy behavior of splitting on spaces and/or commas.' \
        '# Valid values for version_path_separator are:' \
        '#' \
        '# version_path_separator = :' \
        '# version_path_separator = ;' \
        '# version_path_separator = space' \
        'version_path_separator = os  # Use os.pathsep. Default configuration used for new projects.' \
        '' \
        '# set to '\''true'\'' to search source files recursively' \
        '# in each "version_locations" directory' \
        '# new in Alembic version 1.10' \
        '# recursive_version_locations = false' \
        '' \
        '# the output encoding used when revision files' \
        '# are written from script.py.mako' \
        '# output_encoding = utf-8' \
        '' \
        '#sqlalchemy.url = driver://user:pass@localhost/dbname' \
        '' \
        '' \
        '[post_write_hooks]' \
        '# post_write_hooks defines scripts or Python functions that are run' \
        '# on newly generated revision scripts.  See the documentation for further' \
        '# detail and examples' \
        '' \
        '# format using "black" - use the console_scripts runner, against the "black" entrypoint' \
        '# hooks = black' \
        '# black.type = console_scripts' \
        '# black.entrypoint = black' \
        '# black.options = -l 79 REVISION_SCRIPT_FILENAME' \
        '' \
        '# lint with attempts to fix using "ruff" - use the exec runner, execute a binary' \
        '# hooks = ruff' \
        '# ruff.type = exec' \
        '# ruff.executable = %(here)s/.venv/bin/ruff' \
        '# ruff.options = --fix REVISION_SCRIPT_FILENAME' \
        > "$DBGPT_APP_DIR/pilot_template/meta_data/alembic.ini" \
    && printf '%s\n' \
        'from alembic import context' \
        'from sqlalchemy import engine_from_config, pool' \
        '' \
        'from dbgpt.storage.metadata.db_manager import db' \
        '' \
        '# this is the Alembic Config object, which provides' \
        '# access to the values within the .ini file in use.' \
        'config = context.config' \
        '' \
        '' \
        '# add your model'\''s MetaData object here' \
        '# for '\''autogenerate'\'' support' \
        '# from myapp import mymodel' \
        '' \
        '' \
        '# other values from the config, defined by the needs of env.py,' \
        '# can be acquired:' \
        '# my_important_option = config.get_main_option("my_important_option")' \
        '# ... etc.' \
        '' \
        '' \
        'def run_migrations_offline() -> None:' \
        '    """Run migrations in '\''offline'\'' mode.' \
        '' \
        '    This configures the context with just a URL' \
        '    and not an Engine, though an Engine is acceptable' \
        '    here as well.  By skipping the Engine creation' \
        '    we don'\''t even need a DBAPI to be available.' \
        '' \
        '    Calls to context.execute() here emit the given string to the' \
        '    script output.' \
        '' \
        '    """' \
        '    target_metadata = db.metadata' \
        '    url = config.get_main_option("sqlalchemy.url")' \
        '    assert target_metadata is not None' \
        '    assert url is not None' \
        '    context.configure(' \
        '        url=url,' \
        '        target_metadata=target_metadata,' \
        '        literal_binds=True,' \
        '        dialect_opts={"paramstyle": "named"},' \
        '    )' \
        '' \
        '    with context.begin_transaction():' \
        '        context.run_migrations()' \
        '' \
        '' \
        'def run_migrations_online() -> None:' \
        '    """Run migrations in '\''online'\'' mode.' \
        '' \
        '    In this scenario we need to create an Engine' \
        '    and associate a connection with the context.' \
        '' \
        '    """' \
        '    engine = db.engine' \
        '    target_metadata = db.metadata' \
        '    with engine.connect() as connection:' \
        '        if engine.dialect.name == "sqlite":' \
        '            context.configure(' \
        '                connection=engine.connect(),' \
        '                target_metadata=target_metadata,' \
        '                render_as_batch=True,' \
        '            )' \
        '        else:' \
        '            context.configure(connection=connection, target_metadata=target_metadata)' \
        '' \
        '        with context.begin_transaction():' \
        '            context.run_migrations()' \
        '' \
        '' \
        'if context.is_offline_mode():' \
        '    run_migrations_offline()' \
        'else:' \
        '    run_migrations_online()' \
        > "$DBGPT_APP_DIR/pilot_template/meta_data/alembic/env.py" \
    && printf '%s\n' \
        '"""${message}' \
        '' \
        'Revision ID: ${up_revision}' \
        'Revises: ${down_revision | comma,n}' \
        'Create Date: ${create_date}' \
        '' \
        '"""' \
        'from typing import Sequence, Union' \
        '' \
        'from alembic import op' \
        'import sqlalchemy as sa' \
        '${imports if imports else ""}' \
        '' \
        '# revision identifiers, used by Alembic.' \
        'revision: str = ${repr(up_revision)}' \
        'down_revision: Union[str, None] = ${repr(down_revision)}' \
        'branch_labels: Union[str, Sequence[str], None] = ${repr(branch_labels)}' \
        'depends_on: Union[str, Sequence[str], None] = ${repr(depends_on)}' \
        '' \
        '' \
        'def upgrade() -> None:' \
        '    ${upgrades if upgrades else "pass"}' \
        '' \
        '' \
        'def downgrade() -> None:' \
        '    ${downgrades if downgrades else "pass"}' \
        > "$DBGPT_APP_DIR/pilot_template/meta_data/alembic/script.py.mako"

# NON-INTERACTIVE STARTUP CONFIG -- baked in as a KNOWN-SAFE DEFAULT, not a
# real deployment config (Story 11.2/11.3 owns wiring this sidecar to real
# credentials and the shared Postgres/Celery surface; this story only has to
# make the container build, boot, and reach a healthy state on its own).
# `disable_alembic_upgrade` is deliberately OMITTED (DB-GPT's own default is
# `false`) now that the `pilot_template/` fix above makes the real migration
# path work -- see that RUN step's own comment.
#
# The one remaining hard requirement to boot at all, confirmed live
# investigating this story (reproduced with `pixi run -e dbgpt-sidecar dbgpt
# start webserver -y`): at least one `[[models.llms]]` entry. With zero
# configured, `dbgpt.model.cluster.worker.manager.
# initialize_worker_manager_in_client`'s local-mode branch never calls
# `_start_local_worker`, leaving the global worker-manager wrapper's inner
# `.worker_manager` `None`, and the very next line (`worker_manager.
# after_start(...)`) crashes with `AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no
# attribute 'after_start'` (reproduced live). A `proxy/*` provider entry
# satisfies this at BOOT time without ever making a network call -- proxy
# adapters only reach out on an actual chat request, never during startup.
#
# Every `models.llms` value below is overridable at `docker run`/compose
# time via the `${env:VAR:-default}` interpolation DB-GPT's own TOML loader
# supports natively (the same mechanism its own
# `configs/dbgpt-proxy-openai.toml` example uses) -- an operator wires a
# real provider by setting `DBGPT_LLM_*`, no rebuild needed. This is DB-GPT's
# OWN runtime interpolation, evaluated when the app reads the file, not
# build-time shell/Docker substitution -- so, like `entrypoint.sh` above,
# this is a fixed literal with no build-time secret to leak.
# `sidecar-placeholder-not-a-real-key` is exactly that: a LOCAL DEV/TEST
# PLACEHOLDER (matching `compose.yml`'s own convention for
# `DJANGO_SECRET_KEY`/`POSTGRES_PASSWORD`), never a real credential, and
# never invoked by anything this story's own build/health verification does.
#
# `[service.web.database].path` BELOW ("pilot/meta_data/dbgpt.db") IS A BARE
# RELATIVE STRING, but -- UNLIKE the `BenchmarkDataManager` `cache_dir` fix
# the `WORKDIR /app/.home` line above this exists for -- it is NOT resolved
# against the process's CWD. Traced into the installed package
# (`dbgpt/configs/model_config.py::_detect_root_path`/`resolve_root_path`):
# for a pip/conda install (no `pyproject.toml` at the detected repo-root
# candidate), `ROOT_PATH` resolves to `$DBGPT_HOME/workspace` if
# `DBGPT_HOME` is set, else `os.path.expanduser("~/.dbgpt")/workspace` --
# i.e. it depends on `$HOME` (this image never sets `DBGPT_HOME`), not on
# WORKDIR. `resolve_root_path()` then joins this relative `path` onto that
# `ROOT_PATH`, so the file actually opens at
# `$HOME/.dbgpt/workspace/pilot/meta_data/dbgpt.db` -- confirmed live via
# `docker exec ... os.walk('/app/.home')`, which found the real file at
# exactly `/app/.home/.dbgpt/workspace/pilot/meta_data/dbgpt.db`, not
# `/app/.home/pilot/meta_data/dbgpt.db` as this relative string alone would
# suggest. A future change to `$HOME` (or setting `$DBGPT_HOME`) silently
# relocates this file; a WORKDIR-only change would NOT (they are only the
# same value here by coincidence). `compose.yml`'s `dbgpt` service volume
# mount targets this real, confirmed path.
# `[service.web.database]` STAYS SQLITE -- PERMANENT, not interim (Story
# 11.2 investigated pointing this at the shared PostgreSQL and found a
# genuine, dual-confirmed upstream limitation: DB-GPT's own app/CLI layer
# hardcodes SQLite/MySQL/OceanBase as the only supported metadata-store
# backends, and working around that gate via DB-GPT's own public
# `db.create_all()` API fails outright with a PostgreSQL DDL syntax error --
# their own SQLAlchemy models use MySQL-specific `TEXT(length)` columns.
# Fixing this would mean forking DB-GPT's own model classes, which AD-9
# forbids. Full citation: `src/platform/compose/compose.yml`'s `dbgpt`
# service `volumes:` comment and `dbgpt_integration/apps.py`'s docstring.
#
# The `models.llms` overrides below WERE verified for real (Story 11.2):
# `docker compose`'s `dbgpt` service now passes through
# `DBGPT_LLM_MODEL_NAME`/`DBGPT_LLM_MODEL_PROVIDER`/`DBGPT_LLM_API_BASE`/
# `DBGPT_LLM_API_KEY` from the host when set (`compose.yml`'s own comment);
# a real Google Gemini key routed through `proxy/openai` pointed at Gemini's
# OpenAI-compatible endpoint produced a genuine, correct text-to-SQL round
# trip end-to-end through `dbgpt_integration/tasks.py`.
RUN printf '%s\n' \
    '[system]' \
    'language = "en"' \
    '' \
    '[service.web]' \
    'host = "0.0.0.0"' \
    'port = 5670' \
    '' \
    '[service.web.database]' \
    'type = "sqlite"' \
    'path = "pilot/meta_data/dbgpt.db"' \
    '' \
    '[[models.llms]]' \
    'name = "${env:DBGPT_LLM_MODEL_NAME:-gpt-4o}"' \
    'provider = "${env:DBGPT_LLM_MODEL_PROVIDER:-proxy/openai}"' \
    'api_base = "${env:DBGPT_LLM_API_BASE:-https://api.openai.com/v1}"' \
    'api_key = "${env:DBGPT_LLM_API_KEY:-sidecar-placeholder-not-a-real-key}"' \
    > /app/dbgpt-sidecar-config.toml

# Pre-create the sqlite state directory (`[service.web.database].path` above
# actually resolves to `/app/.home/.dbgpt/workspace/pilot/meta_data/dbgpt.db`
# -- see that config line's own comment for why it is NOT the
# `/app/.home/pilot/meta_data/...` the bare relative string would suggest)
# BEFORE the group-write fixup below, not left for the app to create on
# first boot: `compose.yml`'s `dbgpt` service mounts a named volume at this
# exact path for durability across `docker compose down && up` (permanent,
# not interim -- Story 11.2 found real PostgreSQL/`dbgpt_schema` wiring for
# this store structurally impossible; see that file's own comment). Docker
# initializes a fresh named volume from
# whatever already exists at the image mount point, INCLUDING permissions --
# so this directory has to already carry the same group-0-writable posture
# as the rest of `/app/.home`, or the volume-backed mount would come up
# root-owned and unwritable by the arbitrary runtime uid.
RUN mkdir -p /app/.home/.dbgpt/workspace/pilot/meta_data

# Arbitrary-UID / rootless setup -- same posture as `src/platform/Containerfile`
# (GID 0, `chgrp 0`/`chmod g+rwX` on exactly the one path written at
# runtime). No `STATIC_ROOT`-equivalent here: this image ships no
# generated visitor-facing assets to protect the way the main image's
# `staticfiles/` does -- DB-GPT's own frontend bundle is read-only, baked
# into the pixi-materialized `dbgpt_app` package under `.pixi/envs/`, same
# as every other conda-sourced file in this image. `/app` itself is
# deliberately NOT group-writable, for the same POSIX-unlink reason as the
# main image (see its own comment).
RUN chgrp -R 0 /app/.home \
    && chmod -R g+rwX /app/.home

# USER 1001:0 -- an arbitrary example non-root uid paired with GID 0, same
# convention (and the same "1001 itself carries no significance" caveat) as
# `src/platform/Containerfile`.
USER 1001:0

# Declares the port `CMD`'s webserver actually binds (`service.web.port`
# above) -- pure metadata, matches `src/platform/Containerfile`'s own EXPOSE
# rationale.
EXPOSE 5670

ENTRYPOINT ["/app/entrypoint.sh"]
CMD ["dbgpt", "start", "webserver", "-y", "-c", "/app/dbgpt-sidecar-config.toml"]
